# WHAT MUST NOT GO INTO THE IMAGE.
#
# This is a security control, not a build-time optimisation. `COPY . /app` copies the build context
# verbatim, and the build context is this working tree -- which on a live machine holds
# data/auth.json (the operator token, which is shell access to the host), data/llm-provider.json (paid
# API keys), latch.log (the tailnet address the secret scanner already flags), and .git (every secret
# ever committed and later reverted, still reachable by hash).
#
# An image layer is not private. It is content-addressed, cached, pushed to registries, and readable by
# anyone who can pull it -- `docker history` plus a tar extract is enough, no running container needed.
# Nor can a secret be removed by deleting it in a later layer: the earlier layer still carries it.
#
# DENY FIRST, then admit. A gitignore-shaped denylist is the wrong way round here, because the risk is
# the file nobody thought to name. The two failure modes are not symmetric:
#   - allowlist too narrow  -> the image fails to start. Loud, immediate, fixed in minutes.
#   - denylist too narrow   -> a credential is baked into a layer and possibly pushed. Silent.
# Only one of those is recoverable, so the build is arranged to break rather than to leak.
#
# The admitted list below is derived, not guessed: `git ls-files` for top-level JS, plus every local
# import in server.js and its four siblings, checked on 2026-08-20. There are no dynamic imports, so
# static reading is sufficient. If a new top-level module appears, the first build after it will fail
# with MODULE_NOT_FOUND naming exactly what is missing.
*

# --- runtime, admitted deliberately -----------------------------------------------------------------
!package.json
!server.js
!email.mjs
!github.mjs
!mcp.mjs
!schedule.mjs
!public/

# AGPL-3.0: building and handing someone an image is conveying the program, and section 4 requires the
# licence text to travel with it. Omitting this would be a licence violation, not untidiness.
!LICENSE

# --- re-excluded INSIDE the admitted trees -----------------------------------------------------------
# The `!public/` line above admits that directory wholesale, so anything sensitive that ever lands
# inside it needs naming here. These cost nothing and close the case where a log or a dotenv is written
# into a served directory.
**/data/
**/data-dev/
**/*.log
**/*.log.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/node_modules/
